A Dimensional Theory of Housing Affordability: Measurement Admissibility, Falsifiable Thresholds, and Propagated Uncertainty in a Three-County Comparison

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM


Abstract

Comparisons of housing affordability across locations routinely conflate quantities that are not dimensionally commensurable and constructs that are not measurement-equivalent. A typical comparison sets a modeled smoothed home-value index against an administrative 40th-percentile gross-rent benchmark, or a quality-adjusted repeat-rent index against a median asking rent on new leases, and treats the resulting gap as a signal — of regional divergence, of policy failure, of cost-of-living. The gap is not a signal. It is, in the formal sense, undefined: a difference of a stock and a flow, or of two constructs with distinct statistical targets, has no admissible interpretation without an explicitly declared conversion operator.

This thesis develops a dimensional theory of housing affordability in which the admissibility constraints that govern such comparisons are stated as axioms of the theory rather than as data-cleaning conventions. Two axioms are imposed. Axiom 1 (dimensional admissibility) forbids subtraction, summation, or inequality between quantities of distinct dimension — flow ($\$$/yr or $\$$/mo), stock ($\$$), ratio (dimensionless), duration, and horizon — unless a yield, price-to-rent, or amortization operator is declared. Axiom 2 (measure-type tagging) forbids aggregation across measurement constructs without a stated conversion. The affordability object is the residual budget $\rho_{\ell,i} = Y_{d,i} - R_{\ell,i}$, where $R_{\ell,i} = \sum_k X^{(k)}_{\ell,i}$ is the aggregate recurring cost over childcare $G$, housing $H$, transport $T$, debt service $M$, and property tax $TX$, and $Y_{d,i}$ is disposable income.

Under controlled comparison in which all components of $\mathbf{X}$ other than $G$ are held constant across two locations, the theory yields a falsifiable insolvency threshold: $\rho_{\mathrm{MA},i} < 0 \iff \rho_{\mathrm{OH},i} < \Delta G$, with $\Delta G = 12{,}563\,\$/\mathrm{yr} = 1{,}046.92\,\$/\mathrm{mo}$ in the verified data. The framework further delivers: a first-order ratio delta-method for the variance of the burden ratio $B_H = H / Y_d$, which renders the naive additive rule $\sigma^2_{B_H} = \sigma_H^2 + \sigma_{Y_d}^2$ inadmissible; a variance decomposition for $R$ in which the independence hypothesis is a substantive, falsifiable claim about the covariance structure of $(G, H, T)$; a proof that the Census API sentinel returned in non-calculable margin-of-error fields contaminates the aggregate variance by seven or more orders of magnitude if it is not mapped to $\mathrm{NaN}$ before the conversion $\sigma = \mathrm{MOE}/1.645$; a proof that substituting a count from the C01 series for a share from the C03 series in ACS table S0801 injects an error of order of the tract population — five to six orders of magnitude larger than the intended quantity — and that the error is silent against every downstream validity check; an exact rate differential $\Delta M \approx 672.5\,\$/\mathrm{mo}$ at $Q = 324{,}000$, $r_0 = 3.94\%$, $r_1 = 7.24\%$, $n = 360$, which corrects a stated $\sim 710$ that in fact corresponds to $Q \approx 342{,}300$; a proof that county-median effective property-tax rates are biased substitutes for parcel-level rates whenever assessed value and effective rate are correlated, as in Franklin County OH; and a non-identifiability result for the capacity object $J_i = 30 L_i / D_i$, which forbids all policy inference from the capacity side until $L_i$ and $D_i$ are measured with their own uncertainty.

The empirical setting is a three-location comparison across Franklin County OH, Maricopa County AZ, and Suffolk County MA, using verified Zillow ZHVI and HUD FMR data for fiscal year 2026, and ACS 5-Year 2020–2024 extraction on the three FIPS pairs $(39, 049)$, $(04, 013)$, $(25, 025)$. The framework is falsifiable at the level of point estimates, of propagated uncertainty, and of the covariance sign pattern that the independence hypothesis excludes.

