Appendix A — Dimensional Algebra and Admissibility Conditions

A.1 The dimension set

Let $\mathcal{D} = \{\mathsf{F},\ \mathsf{S},\ \mathsf{R},\ \mathsf{D},\ \mathsf{H}\}$ denote the set of primitive dimensions, with intended interpretations

\begin{aligned}
\mathsf{F} &= \text{flow}, && \$/\mathrm{yr} \ \text{or}\ \$/\mathrm{mo},\\
\mathsf{S} &= \text{stock}, && \$,\\
\mathsf{R} &= \text{ratio}, && \text{dimensionless},\\
\mathsf{D} &= \text{duration}, && \text{months or days},\\
\mathsf{H} &= \text{horizon}, && \text{dimensionless count}.
\end{aligned}

A dimensioned quantity is a pair $q = (\nu, d)$ with numerical value $\nu \in \mathbb{R}$ and dimension $d \in \mathcal{D}$. The dimension map is $\dim(q) = d$. Binary operations on dimensioned quantities are defined only when the dimensions agree:

q_1 \pm q_2 \in \mathcal{D}\text{-admissible} \iff \dim(q_1) = \dim(q_2),


q_1 \cdot q_2 \text{ is always defined}, \qquad \dim(q_1 q_2) = \dim(q_1) \otimes \dim(q_2),

where $\otimes$ is the free product on dimensions (e.g. $\mathsf{F} \otimes \mathsf{D} = \mathsf{S}$, since $\$/\mathrm{mo} \times \mathrm{mo} = \$\,$). Division reverses the product: $\dim(q_1/q_2) = \dim(q_1) \oslash \dim(q_2)$.

A.2 The two axioms

Axiom 1 (Dimensional admissibility). For every comparison, sum, or inequality $q_1 \star q_2$ appearing in the theory with $\star \in \{+,-,\le,\ge,<,>\}$, admissibility requires $\dim(q_1) = \dim(q_2)$. If $\dim(q_1) \neq \dim(q_2)$, the expression is undefined and cannot enter any subsequent inference.

Axiom 2 (Measure-type tagging). Every observed quantity $q$ carries a tag $\tau(q) \in \mathcal{T}$, where $\mathcal{T}$ is a finite set of measurement constructs. Two quantities with $\tau(q_1) \neq \tau(q_2)$ are not estimators of a common target, and no aggregation across tags is admissible without a stated conversion operator $\Phi_{\tau_1 \to \tau_2}$ declared in advance.

A.3 Consequences

Lemma A.1 (Stock–flow non-admissibility). Let $q_{\mathsf{S}}$ be a stock quantity and $q_{\mathsf{F}}$ a flow quantity. Then $q_{\mathsf{S}} - q_{\mathsf{F}}$ is undefined under Axiom 1, and no averaging, deflation, or ranking operation on such expressions is admissible.

Proof. By definition $\dim(q_{\mathsf{S}}) = \mathsf{S}$ and $\dim(q_{\mathsf{F}}) = \mathsf{F}$, and $\mathsf{S} \neq \mathsf{F}$ in $\mathcal{D}$. Axiom 1 forbids the subtraction directly. Since any subsequent operation (averaging, ranking, deflation) presupposes the value of $q_{\mathsf{S}} - q_{\mathsf{F}}$, and this value does not exist, those operations are also undefined. $\square$

Lemma A.2 (Construct non-commensurability). Let $q_1, q_2$ be flow-dimension quantities with $\tau(q_1) \neq \tau(q_2)$. Then their difference is not a measurement error of a common latent scalar $\mu$; equivalently, no decomposition $q_j = \mu + \varepsilon_j$ with $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_j] = 0$ exists that would make the average $\tfrac{1}{2}(q_1 + q_2)$ an estimator of $\mu$.

Proof. If $q_j = \mu + \varepsilon_j$ with $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_j] = 0$, then $\mathbb{E}[q_1] = \mathbb{E}[q_2] = \mu$. But by Axiom 2, $\tau(q_1) \neq \tau(q_2)$ implies $\mathbb{E}[q_1] \neq \mathbb{E}[q_2]$ in general, since they estimate distinct statistical targets (e.g. a 40th-percentile administrative benchmark and a quality-adjusted typical asking rent). Hence no such decomposition exists, and the average is not an estimator of a common mean. $\square$

Lemma A.3 (Admissible $\mathsf{S} \to \mathsf{F}$ conversion). The only admissible conversion from stock to flow is multiplication by a declared yield operator $y : \mathsf{S} \to \mathsf{F}$, with $y$ specified by source, date, and construction method. Any undeclared conversion produces a quantity whose dimension is ambiguous and whose value depends on the analyst's implicit assumption.

Proof. By Axiom 1, converting $\mathsf{S} \to \mathsf{F}$ requires multiplication by a quantity of dimension $\mathsf{F} \oslash \mathsf{S} = \mathsf{D}^{-1}$ — a rate. The rate must be declared, or the resulting flow inherits an undefined dimension. By Axiom 2, if the rate is derived from a source whose measure type is unspecified, the resulting flow has ambiguous construct, hence is not admissible as an estimator of any declared flow construct. $\square$

